[{:db/id "q-350548/field-1/894040af4eb4f74d268a1de5813d74686faf1a22690102f0101d53b8bf3e439c" :answer/id #uuid "65338c66-e928-50a3-9616-fbfda716827b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20t^4+10t^9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350548/field-1/bc1e3201c7e1f5484dfa1706a75ff8730b0e59c2b02ccc7f438c0dafca55fff8" :answer/id #uuid "5bd545d1-de75-59e3-a4de-b5e07bb5d71b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20t^4+10t^5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350548/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "b01e1ddf-a21a-5597-a6fc-26a62b71e64a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-350548/field-1/894040af4eb4f74d268a1de5813d74686faf1a22690102f0101d53b8bf3e439c" :answer-field/choices ["q-350548/field-1/894040af4eb4f74d268a1de5813d74686faf1a22690102f0101d53b8bf3e439c" "q-350548/field-1/bc1e3201c7e1f5484dfa1706a75ff8730b0e59c2b02ccc7f438c0dafca55fff8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-350548" :question/id #uuid "11d0068f-c50c-56c9-ac9d-c760f1b21cef" :question/math-academy-id "q-350548" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+{t}^{5})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+{t}^{5})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(4x+{t}^{5})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(4x+{t}^{5})^{2}] & =2(4x+{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(4x+{t}^{5}) \\\\  & =2(4x+{t}^{5})\\cdot 5{t}^{4} \\\\  & =10{t}^{4}(4x+{t}^{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}10{t}^{4}(4x+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10{t}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{t}^{5}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2+{t}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=10{t}^{4}(2+{t}^{5}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-350548/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-350548"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-350550/field-1/b046ca2f88edab8492765792146511ac6d7233b61ff927ae179262786f44e60c" :answer/id #uuid "69a0b7ad-9c2f-5f95-bde4-a3524ee2abb9" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}t^3+16t^7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350550/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "d0686e5e-b3bc-5db8-a7fd-a2659198ecb5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-350550/field-1/b046ca2f88edab8492765792146511ac6d7233b61ff927ae179262786f44e60c" :answer-field/choices ["q-350550/field-1/b046ca2f88edab8492765792146511ac6d7233b61ff927ae179262786f44e60c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-350550" :question/id #uuid "47e13c43-3d7e-5887-8a06-229459d7d60b" :question/math-academy-id "q-350550" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})^{2}] & =2({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\cdot 4{t}^{3} \\\\  & =8{t}^{3}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}8{t}^{3}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{t}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{0}^{2}+{t}^{4}x{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}+2{t}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=8{t}^{3}(\\frac{8}{3}+2{t}^{4}).\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-350550/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-350550"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-350551/field-1/8d4191e6e8cc3013d0ef84c8ea4953971358fff0f0060734825100d0f3acfd8d" :answer/id #uuid "826bad29-8cab-576e-8fe5-c67f43909d9d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "20t^9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-350551/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "ce38428c-6c3f-5e4a-8f35-9675de041abc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-350551/field-1/8d4191e6e8cc3013d0ef84c8ea4953971358fff0f0060734825100d0f3acfd8d" :answer-field/choices ["q-350551/field-1/8d4191e6e8cc3013d0ef84c8ea4953971358fff0f0060734825100d0f3acfd8d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-350551" :question/id #uuid "c72f5578-6a77-5982-9416-5354dbedb9f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-350551" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}] & =2(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5}) \\\\  & =2(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\cdot 5{t}^{4} \\\\  & =10{t}^{4}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{4}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10{t}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{t}^{5}x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-1)+({t}^{5}-(-{t}^{5})) \\\\  & =2{t}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=10{t}^{4}(2{t}^{5})=20{t}^{9}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-350551/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-350551"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
